LLM + Google Maps Backend (Technical Test)
Overview

This project is a backend service built with FastAPI that integrates an LLM (Large Language Model) with a Google Maps search feature.
It was prepared as part of the technical test for HeyPico.ai.

The service provides endpoints to:

Query Google Maps Places API (or a simulated fallback if no API key is available).

Generate responses from the LLM and integrate them with search results.

Important Note on Google Maps API Key

Google Maps API requires a billing-enabled account in order to issue a valid API key.
Since I currently do not have the ability to enable billing, the project does not use a real Google Maps API key.

What I did instead:

Implemented a fallback mechanism that simulates Google Maps responses.

If no API key is provided, the service returns a result with:

A placeholder name (e.g., "Search result for: <query>").

A default Google Maps search link (https://www.google.com/maps/search/?q=\
<query>).

This ensures the backend logic works end-to-end without requiring billing.
The fallback keeps the system functional while demonstrating the integration design.

Project Structure
llm-gmaps-backend/
│── app/
│   ├── routes/        # API routes
│   ├── services/      # Business logic (LLM + Maps)
│   ├── main.py        # FastAPI entrypoint
│   ├── config.py      # Configuration & API key (optional)
│   └── models.py      # Data models
│
│── tests/             # Unit tests
│── venv/              # Python virtual environment
│── requirements.txt   # Dependencies


Setup Instructions

1. Clone repository & install dependencies:
python -m venv venv
venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt


2. Run the backend:
uvicorn app.main:app --reload --port 8000


3. Test API in VS Code (REST Client) or Postman:
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/search?query=coffee%20shop


Clarification for HeyPico.ai Reviewers
- LLM Integration: The backend successfully integrates an LLM API endpoint (/generate).
- Maps Integration: Since a real Google Maps API key requires billing, I used a mock/fallback response.
- Goal: The implementation demonstrates the design, integration logic, and automation workflow that would work identically with a real API key.
If a real Google Maps API Key is provided, the service will call the actual Places API without code changes.

Next Steps (With a Valid API Key)
To enable full functionality:
1. Create a Google Cloud Project.
2. Enable the Places API.
3. Add your API key to config.py: GOOGLE_API_KEY = "your_api_key_here"
4. Restart the server → real Google Maps data will be returned.

Thank you for reviewing this submission.
The project is designed with flexibility so it can run both in mock mode (no billing required) and real API mode (with a valid key).